/**
 * seo/topic-intros.js — original opening paragraphs for the /verses-about/
 * topic pages, written for TheWay.
 *
 * Why this file exists: a topic page is a list of King James verses, and the
 * King James text is public domain. Without an intro, every one of these 290
 * pages says the same boilerplate sentence and is indistinguishable from the
 * dozens of other sites running the same list. This is the only original
 * writing on a topic page, and it is what an AI assistant quotes when somebody
 * asks what the Bible says about a subject.
 *
 * Keyed by topic slug. A topic with no entry falls back to the generic line,
 * so this can be filled in a batch at a time.
 *
 * Batch 1 (Sept 19): the 40 topics with the most search demand — the queries
 * Search Console already shows impressions for ("bible verses about marriage",
 * "bible verses about hope") plus the perennial ones.
 * Batch 2 (Sept 19): God's character and the sin/character topics — the next
 * tier of demand, and the ones an assistant is most likely to be asked about.
 * Batch 3 (Sept 19): life events, seasons and holidays — the topics people
 *          search for a card, a ceremony or a hard week rather than a study.
 * Batch 4 (Sept 19): people of the Bible. These read differently from the
 *          thematic ones — who the person was and where the story sits,
 *          closer in shape to the book-page intros.
 * Batch 5 (Sept 19): the stories and teachings — parables, miracles, the
 *          events of Christ's life, and the remaining New Testament figures.
 * Batch 6 (Sept 19): the remainder — the names of God, the last of the people,
 *          and the practical topics. All 290 topic pages now carry original
 *          copy; no page falls back to the generic line any more.
 */
export const TOPIC_INTROS = {
  "fear-and-anxiety": "Fear turns up constantly in scripture, and almost never as something to be ashamed of. The command not to be afraid usually comes with a reason attached, and the reason is God rather than the size of the thing in front of you. These verses run from Isaiah to Philippians.",
  "hope": "Hope in the Bible isn't wishing. It's expectation with something solid underneath it, a promise made by somebody who keeps promises. Most of these verses were written to people in exile, in prison, or waiting on something that hadn't come yet, which is why they hold up when a good mood won't.",
  "marriage": "Scripture talks about marriage as a covenant before it talks about it as a feeling. These verses cover what husbands and wives are each told to do, what Jesus said about two becoming one flesh, and what Paul meant by love that suffereth long — the passages read at weddings and the ones that matter afterward.",
  "strength": "The Bible almost never tells a strong person to be strong. These verses land on the tired, the outnumbered and the afraid: Joshua taking over from Moses, Paul with a thorn he couldn't shake, Isaiah writing to people who had nothing left. The strength promised in them comes from outside the person needing it.",
  "healing": "Scripture treats healing as something God does, sometimes through ordinary means and sometimes without them, and it never pretends the sick are at fault. These verses include the ones people pray over a hospital bed and the harder ones about a healing that waits.",
  "gods-love": "God's love in scripture is shown before it is described. It shows up as a Son given, a shepherd going after one sheep, a father running down a road. These verses gather what the Bible says about a love that started first and doesn't rise and fall with how the week went.",
  "faith": "Faith in the Bible isn't believing without a reason. Hebrews calls it substance and evidence, which are solid words. These verses cover what faith is, where it comes from, and what it looks like when somebody acts on what God said before they can see how it turns out.",
  "peace": "Two kinds of peace run through scripture: peace with God, which was settled at the cross, and the peace of God, which keeps guard over a mind while the trouble is still going. These verses cover both, including the ones Jesus spoke the night before he was arrested.",
  "grief-and-loss": "The Bible gives grief room. David wrote songs in it. Jesus wept at a grave he was about to open. Paul told the Thessalonians they would sorrow, just not as people with no hope. These verses sit with loss rather than hurrying anybody past it.",
  "encouragement": "Encouragement in scripture is usually specific — a name, a promise, a reason. These are the verses people send when somebody is worn out: the ones about God going on ahead, holding on, and finishing what he started.",
  "forgiveness": "Forgiveness runs both directions in scripture, and the verses hold the two together on purpose: forgive as you have been forgiven. These cover what God does with confessed sin, what Jesus said about the limit Peter tried to set, and what to do when the other person hasn't asked.",
  "depression": "Scripture doesn't scold people who are low. Elijah asked to die and was fed and let to sleep. David asked his own soul why it was cast down, out loud, more than once. These verses meet discouragement plainly, and many of them were written by people in the middle of it.",
  "protection": "Protection in the Bible is usually pictured rather than stated in the abstract: a refuge, a shield, a high tower, wings. These verses gather those images, including the whole of Psalm 91, along with the ones where protection meant God with somebody in the trouble rather than out of it.",
  "trusting-god": "Trust in scripture is something you lean your weight on. Proverbs sets it against leaning on your own understanding, which isn't an insult to your thinking. It's a statement about how much your thinking can hold. These verses cover trusting God with what you can't see yet.",
  "patience-and-endurance": "The King James word is often longsuffering, which says it better — patience that has to last a while. These verses cover waiting on God, running a race with patience, and the promise in Galatians that the harvest comes if you don't faint first.",
  "money-and-finances": "Scripture talks about money more than most people expect, and it is rarely flattering or condemning on its own. These verses cover giving, debt, contentment, the love of money Paul called a root of evil, and what Jesus said about where a treasure and a heart end up together.",
  "work": "The Bible puts work before the fall, not after it, which changes how the rest of it reads. These verses cover doing it heartily as to the Lord, resting from it, working under a hard employer, and the dignity Paul gave to ordinary labor.",
  "friendship": "Scripture's picture of friendship is loyalty more than company. A friend loveth at all times. Iron sharpeneth iron. Jonathan kept faith with David when it cost him a throne. These verses cover the friendships in the Bible and what they say a real one does.",
  "family": "The Bible's families are not tidy, and it doesn't hide it. These verses cover what is asked of parents, children, husbands and wives, and the households that chose to serve the Lord, alongside the promise that God sets the solitary in families.",
  "children-and-parenting": "Scripture gives parents instruction and comfort in about equal measure. These verses cover training a child, provoking them and not, teaching at home in ordinary hours, and what Jesus said when the disciples tried to keep children away from him.",
  "thankfulness": "Thanksgiving in scripture usually comes before the situation changes rather than after. Paul writes it from prison, and the psalms sing it in the middle of trouble. These verses cover giving thanks in everything, and what the Bible says that does to a person.",
  "prayer": "The Bible teaches prayer mostly by example — people talking to God plainly, including when they are angry. These verses cover how Jesus told his disciples to pray, praying without ceasing, and what happens to worry when it gets said out loud to God.",
  "joy": "Joy in scripture isn't tied to circumstances, which is why so much of it was written by people in bad ones. These verses cover the joy of the LORD as strength, joy that comes in the morning, and the joy Jesus said he wanted his followers to have full.",
  "wisdom": "Wisdom in the Bible begins with the fear of the LORD, which Proverbs treats as a starting point rather than a summary. These verses cover asking God for it, the difference between knowing things and being wise, and what James says God does with anybody who asks.",
  "temptation": "Scripture treats temptation as ordinary and survivable. Paul says none of it is new and that God makes a way out. These verses cover how Jesus answered it in the wilderness, what James says about where it starts, and why fleeing is sometimes the instruction.",
  "courage": "Courage in the Bible rests on a fact rather than a feeling. Be strong and of a good courage comes with the LORD thy God is with thee attached to it. These verses cover the people who were told that and what they had to walk into afterward.",
  "comfort": "The word for comfort in scripture is closer to coming alongside than to soothing. Paul says God comforts us so that we can comfort others with the same comfort we were given. These verses cover that, and the ones people reach for at a graveside.",
  "loneliness": "The Bible says more about being alone than people expect. God called it not good at the very beginning. Elijah was sure he was the only one left and was told otherwise. These verses cover God's presence with people who feel unseen, including the promise never to leave.",
  "worry": "Jesus spent a long stretch of the Sermon on the Mount on worry, and his argument is mostly about birds and flowers, things fed and clothed without anxious effort. These verses cover that passage, Paul's instruction to pray instead of carry, and Peter's line about casting care.",
  "stress": "Scripture doesn't use the word stress, but it is full of people carrying more than they can. Moses was told to share the load. Martha was told her sister had chosen the better part. These verses cover rest, burdens handed off, and the yoke Jesus called easy.",
  "women": "The Bible's women are prophets, judges, queens, business owners and the first people at the empty tomb. These verses gather what scripture says to and about them, including Proverbs 31 read as a whole rather than as a checklist.",
  "men": "Scripture's instruction to men is mostly about how they treat people — wives, children, workers, enemies. These verses cover strength and gentleness held together, the charge Paul gave a young Timothy, and what is asked of a husband and a father.",
  "leadership": "Leadership in the Bible is defined downward. The greatest among you shall be your servant, said to men arguing about rank. These verses cover shepherding rather than lording, the qualifications Paul listed, and the leaders scripture holds up next to the ones it doesn't.",
  "giving-and-generosity": "Scripture ties giving to the heart behind it more than to the amount. A widow's two mites outweighed everything else put in the treasury that day. These verses cover cheerful giving, the tithe, caring for the poor, and what Jesus said about doing it quietly.",
  "humility": "Humility in the Bible isn't thinking poorly of yourself. It's being the right size. God resisteth the proud and giveth grace to the humble. These verses cover Christ emptying himself, the washing of feet, and the promise that God lifts up whoever is willing to go low.",
  "salvation": "These are the verses that say plainly how a person is saved: by grace, through faith, not of works. They cover what Paul wrote to the Romans and the Ephesians, what Jesus told Nicodemus at night, and what Peter preached to a crowd that asked what they should do.",
  "heaven-and-eternity": "Scripture says less about heaven's scenery than people expect and more about who is there. These verses cover the place Jesus said he was going to prepare, the city John saw, the end of tears and death, and what the Bible means when it says for ever.",
  "the-armor-of-god": "Paul lists six pieces in Ephesians 6, and every one of them is worn rather than carried into a fight you picked. These verses cover the whole passage — the belt, breastplate, shoes, shield, helmet and sword — and the line about wrestling not against flesh and blood.",
  "spiritual-warfare": "The Bible's picture of spiritual conflict is defensive more than dramatic. Stand, resist, and submit first. These verses cover the armor in Ephesians, James on resisting the devil, and Paul's reminder that the weapons of this warfare are not carnal.",
  "death": "Scripture calls death an enemy and then says it was swallowed up in victory. These verses cover what the Bible says about dying, what Jesus said standing outside Lazarus's grave, and Paul's question about where death's sting had gone.",
  "grace-and-mercy": "Mercy is not getting what you had coming. Grace is getting what you never had coming. Scripture keeps them side by side, and these verses cover both — the God who is slow to anger, the debt cancelled, and Paul's insistence that if it could be earned it wouldn't be grace at all.",
  "obedience": "Obedience in the Bible follows love rather than replacing it. If ye love me, keep my commandments, in that order. These verses cover what God asks, what Samuel told Saul about obeying being better than sacrifice, and the obedience Jesus himself kept all the way to a cross.",
  "repentance": "Repentance isn't feeling bad. The word means turning — a change of mind that changes direction. These verses cover what God does with a broken and a contrite heart, what John and Jesus both opened their preaching with, and the difference Paul drew between godly sorrow and the other kind.",
  "sin": "Scripture is blunt about sin and never only about other people's. All have sinned is written to religious people. These verses cover what sin is, what it pays, what it does to a person's own sense of things, and what God does with it the moment it's confessed.",
  "holy-spirit": "The Holy Spirit in scripture teaches, convicts, comforts, gives gifts, and produces fruit. These verses cover who he is rather than what he feels like — the promise Jesus made before he left, the power at Pentecost, and what Paul says about grieving him.",
  "jesus": "These are the verses that say who Jesus is in scripture's own words: the Word made flesh, the way and the truth and the life, the lamb of God, the firstborn of every creature. They cover his claims about himself and what the apostles wrote about him afterward.",
  "gods-faithfulness": "The Bible's argument for God's faithfulness is usually history, not sentiment. He kept this before, so he will keep it now. These verses cover mercies new every morning, a God who cannot deny himself, and the promises made to people who had no way to make him keep them.",
  "gods-promises": "Scripture treats a promise from God as something to hold him to. Abraham did. So did Moses and David. These verses gather the promises the Bible repeats most, along with Paul's claim that in Christ they are all yea and amen.",
  "gods-presence": "The Bible's comfort is usually presence before it's rescue. I will be with thee shows up more often than I will get you out. These verses cover God with people in fire, in exile, in a valley, and Jesus' last line in Matthew about being with us always.",
  "gods-power": "Scripture shows God's power at work in creation, in resurrection, and in ordinary people who had none of their own. These verses cover what the Bible says he is able to do, including Paul's line about exceeding abundantly above all that we ask or think.",
  "gods-sovereignty": "Sovereignty means God runs things, including the parts nobody would have chosen. Scripture holds that alongside real human choices without apologizing for the tension. These verses cover his rule over nations, over circumstances, and over what Joseph told his brothers about what they meant for evil.",
  "gods-goodness": "The Bible asks people to test this one: O taste and see that the LORD is good. These verses cover his goodness as something experienced rather than assumed, including the psalms written by men who said it while things were still hard.",
  "scripture-and-gods-word": "These verses are the Bible talking about itself — given by inspiration, quick and powerful, a lamp for the next step. They cover what scripture claims to be, what it is for, and Jesus' own habit of answering with it is written.",
  "worship-and-praise": "Worship in scripture is bigger than music and older than buildings. It shows up as bowing, building altars, dancing, singing, and Paul's line about offering your body as a living sacrifice. These verses cover what the Bible calls worship and who it is aimed at.",
  "spiritual-growth": "Growth in scripture is slow and normal — milk before meat, a seed before a harvest, a work God started and intends to finish. These verses cover what grows a believer and what Peter says to add to your faith.",
  "seeking-god": "Seeking in the Bible comes with a promise attached: those who seek find, if they seek with the whole heart. These verses cover seeking early, seeking first, and the God who Isaiah says is found by people who weren't even looking.",
  "righteousness": "Scripture has two kinds of righteousness in view — the kind people try to produce, which Isaiah calls filthy rags, and the kind God gives. These verses cover both, including what Paul says happened at the cross to trade one for the other.",
  "identity-in-christ": "The New Testament spends more time on who believers are than on what they should do, and puts it in that order on purpose. These verses cover the names scripture gives them: new creature, child of God, chosen, accepted in the beloved.",
  "eternal-life": "Eternal life in scripture starts now rather than at a funeral. Jesus defines it as knowing God, and John says you can know you have it. These verses cover what is promised, who it is promised to, and what it cost.",
  "rest": "Rest in the Bible is commanded before it's earned — God rested first, and then told people to. These verses cover the sabbath, the rest Jesus offers for the soul, and the rest Hebrews says is still waiting.",
  "sickness": "Scripture doesn't treat illness as a verdict on somebody's faith, and Job's friends are in the Bible partly to show that. These verses cover praying for the sick, God's nearness in it, and the comfort written by people who were unwell when they wrote.",
  "suffering": "The Bible never explains suffering away, and its best-known book on the subject ends without an answer to the question. These verses cover what God does in it, what Paul says it produces, and the promise that it doesn't get the last word.",
  "hard-times": "Scripture's promises for hard stretches are mostly about company rather than escape. Through the fire, through the water, through the valley — through is the word that keeps coming back. These verses cover what God says he'll do while it lasts.",
  "waiting-on-god": "Waiting in the Bible is active. It's the kind that keeps expecting him to move, not the kind that gives up and calls it patience. These verses cover renewed strength, waiting on the LORD, and the psalms written by men who were tired of waiting.",
  "doubt": "Scripture handles doubt better than most people expect. Thomas got to touch the wounds. John the Baptist sent men to ask, from prison, and got a kind answer. These verses cover honest questions and what Jude says to do with people who have them.",
  "guilt-and-shame": "The Bible separates guilt, which is about what you did, from shame, which is about who you think you are. These verses cover confession, cleansing, and the line in Romans about there being no condemnation left for people in Christ.",
  "anger": "Scripture doesn't forbid anger outright — be ye angry, and sin not. It puts a clock on it and a limit around it. These verses cover slow to wrath, letting the sun go down, and what Proverbs says a soft answer does.",
  "bitterness": "Hebrews calls bitterness a root, which is the right picture: out of sight, growing, and it comes up eventually. These verses cover letting it go, what Paul lists it alongside, and the forgiveness that is the only real way out of it.",
  "jealousy-and-envy": "Envy in scripture is treated as rot rather than ambition — Proverbs calls it rottenness of the bones. These verses cover coveting, comparison, and what James says shows up wherever envying and strife are.",
  "pride": "Pride is the one sin the Bible says God actively resists. Pride goeth before destruction is a prediction, not an insult. These verses cover what pride does to a person and the grace scripture says is reserved for the humble instead.",
  "gossip": "Proverbs treats gossip as food — talebearer's words go down into the innermost parts. These verses cover the tongue, the damage done with it, and what scripture says about carrying a story that wasn't yours to carry.",
  "judging-others": "Judge not is quoted more often than it's finished. Jesus goes on to talk about a beam and a mote, which makes it about order rather than silence. These verses cover the whole passage and what Paul adds about judging inside the church.",
  "honesty": "Scripture ties honesty to God's own character — lying lips are an abomination, and a just weight is his delight. These verses cover truthfulness in speech, in business, and in the ordinary places where nobody would know.",
  "truth": "Truth in scripture is a person before it's a proposition. Jesus says I am the truth, and then that it sets people free. These verses cover knowing it, walking in it, and worshipping in spirit and in truth.",
  "kindness": "The King James word is often gentleness or tender mercy, and scripture treats it as strength under control rather than softness. These verses cover kindness to neighbors, to enemies, and the kindness of God that Romans says leads people to repentance.",
  "self-control": "Temperance is the last item in the fruit of the Spirit, which places it as something grown rather than forced. These verses cover Proverbs on a city without walls, Paul on running a race, and Peter's list of what to add to faith.",
  "serving-others": "Jesus defined greatness downward and then washed feet to make the point unmistakable. These verses cover serving as the shape of Christian life — the good Samaritan, the least of these, and bearing one another's burdens.",
  "unity": "The New Testament treats unity as something kept rather than manufactured — endeavouring to keep the unity of the Spirit. These verses cover Jesus' prayer the night before he died, Paul on one body, and the psalm about brethren dwelling together.",
  "loving-your-enemies": "This is the command people find hardest, and Jesus gave a reason for it: that ye may be the children of your Father. These verses cover blessing those who curse, overcoming evil with good, and leaving vengeance where scripture says it belongs.",
  "purpose": "Scripture answers the purpose question at two levels — what people are for in general, and what God is doing with one life in particular. These verses cover both, including Jeremiah on being known before birth and Paul on being God's workmanship.",
  "weddings": "These are the passages couples actually read at a ceremony, and a few that fit better than the usual ones. They cover what Genesis says about leaving and cleaving, what Paul asks of a husband, and the love in 1 Corinthians 13 that was written to settle a church fight.",
  "funerals": "Verses read at a graveside carry more weight than they do anywhere else. These cover the shepherd psalm, the room Jesus said he was going to prepare, Paul's question about death's sting, and the promise that God wipes the tears himself.",
  "birthdays": "Scripture doesn't make much of birthdays, but it says a great deal about a life being counted and known. These verses cover numbering our days, being made on purpose, and the mercies that show up new every morning of one.",
  "graduation": "A graduate is being handed a decision more than a diploma. These verses cover direction, courage for something unfamiliar, and the instruction given to young men and women told to step into work that was over their heads.",
  "new-year": "A new year is a good excuse for what scripture asks all year: examine, let go, and go on. These verses cover forgetting what's behind, mercies that arrive each morning, and committing plans to God before making them.",
  "christmas": "These are the passages the season is actually about — Isaiah's child with the impossible names, Luke's account of the shepherds, and John's line about the Word made flesh. They cover the promise and the night it was kept.",
  "easter": "These verses cover the weekend the whole faith rests on: the crucifixion, the borrowed tomb, the stone, and the morning. Paul stakes everything on it in 1 Corinthians 15, and says so in plain terms.",
  "thanksgiving-day": "Scripture's thanksgiving isn't seasonal, but the day is a good time to read it. These verses cover entering his gates with thanksgiving, giving thanks in everything, and the psalms that list reasons rather than just feelings.",
  "valentines-day": "The Bible's best writing on love is mostly about what it does, not what it feels. These verses cover 1 Corinthians 13, Song of Solomon, and the love John says came first and made ours possible.",
  "mothers-day": "These verses cover what scripture says about mothers and to them — Hannah praying, Mary told to hold on to things, the Proverbs 31 woman read as a whole, and the honor the commandments attach to her by name.",
  "fathers-day": "Scripture asks a lot of fathers and gives them a great deal too. These verses cover raising children without provoking them, teaching at home in ordinary hours, and the picture of a father running down a road to meet a son who wasted everything.",
  "a-new-baby": "These are the verses people write in a card for a birth, and the ones worth reading after everyone leaves. They cover being formed and known before anyone had met you, children as a heritage, and Hannah handing hers back.",
  "a-new-job": "Starting somewhere new raises the same questions scripture keeps answering: who's in charge, who's watching, and whether it matters. These verses cover working heartily as to the Lord, establishing a plan, and God going ahead of somebody into unfamiliar ground.",
  "moving": "The Bible is full of people told to go before they were told where. These verses cover Abraham leaving, Israel following a cloud, and the promise of God's presence wherever somebody ends up, which is the part that travels.",
  "retirement": "Scripture never retires anybody from usefulness, though it has a lot to say about rest. These verses cover bearing fruit in old age, the grey head as a crown, and passing what you know to whoever is coming behind you.",
  "anniversaries": "An anniversary is a good day to read the covenant language again. These verses cover the promises marriage is built on, love that bears and endures, and the psalms about God's faithfulness across a long stretch of years.",
  "pregnancy": "These verses cover what scripture says about a child being formed — fearfully and wonderfully made, knit together, known before birth — along with the prayers women in the Bible prayed while waiting.",
  "adoption": "The New Testament borrows adoption to explain what God does with believers, which says something about how it regards the thing itself. These verses cover being received into a family, the Spirit of adoption, and God setting the solitary in families.",
  "singleness": "Paul was single and wrote about it without treating it as a waiting room. These verses cover undivided devotion, contentment in whatever state, and the fact that scripture's picture of a full life never required a spouse.",
  "dating": "The Bible doesn't describe dating, but it has plenty to say about what to look for and what to guard. These verses cover character over appearance, purity, being unequally yoked, and keeping the heart with diligence.",
  "divorce": "Scripture takes divorce seriously and does not treat the people in it as beyond help. These verses cover what Jesus said when he was asked, what Paul added later, and the passages about God's nearness to people whose hearts are broken.",
  "breakups": "The Bible has no verse about a breakup and a great deal about grief, rejection, and starting over. These verses cover God near the brokenhearted, guarding the heart, and the psalms written by men who had been left.",
  "teenagers-and-youth": "Scripture speaks to young people directly rather than talking over them. These verses cover Paul telling Timothy not to let anyone despise his youth, remembering your Creator early, and keeping a way clean by taking heed to God's word.",
  "kids": "These are the short, plain verses children can actually hold on to, along with what scripture says about them. They cover being fearfully and wonderfully made, obeying parents, and Jesus telling the disciples to let them come.",
  "grandparents": "The Bible gives grandparents a job: tell the next generation what you saw. These verses cover passing faith down, children's children as a crown, and Paul's note about the faith that lived in Timothy's grandmother first.",
  "motherhood": "These verses cover the work itself rather than the sentiment — Hannah, Mary, the Proverbs 31 household, and the mothers scripture names for what they did when it was hard.",
  "fatherhood": "Scripture's instruction to fathers is short and specific: don't provoke them, bring them up, teach them as you go about the day. These verses cover that, along with the father-picture Jesus used for God himself.",
  "husbands": "The New Testament's charge to husbands is heavier than the one people usually quote at them. Love your wife as Christ loved the church, which ended at a cross. These verses cover that, dwelling with her in understanding, and what happens to prayers when a man doesn't.",
  "wives": "These verses cover what scripture says to and about wives, including Proverbs 31 read as a whole rather than as a list of demands, and Peter's line about a meek and quiet spirit being of great price.",
  "military-and-soldiers": "The Bible has soldiers in it who were commended, and it borrows their language for the Christian life. These verses cover courage, endurance, the armor Paul described, and the prayers of people whose work puts them in danger.",
  "missions": "Missions in scripture starts with God rather than with a volunteer — he sends. These verses cover the great commission, Isaiah saying here am I, and Paul's question about how anyone hears without a preacher.",
  "evangelism": "Scripture treats telling people as ordinary rather than specialized. These verses cover being ready to give an answer, the gospel Paul was not ashamed of, and Jesus sending ordinary men out with it before they had it all figured out.",
  "the-great-commission": "The last thing Jesus said before he left was an assignment with a promise attached to it. These verses cover Matthew 28, the parallel accounts, and what the apostles did with it starting in Acts.",
  "baptism": "These verses cover what baptism is in scripture, who received it, and what it pictures. They include Jesus' own baptism, the commission, and Paul's explanation of being buried and raised with Christ.",
  "communion": "These verses cover the Lord's supper as scripture gives it — the night it was instituted, Paul's instructions to a church doing it badly, and what the bread and the cup are meant to say until he comes.",
  "fasting": "The Bible assumes fasting rather than commanding it. When ye fast, said Jesus, not if. These verses cover how he told people to do it, the fast Isaiah says God actually wants, and the times scripture pairs it with prayer.",
  "meditation": "Biblical meditation fills the mind rather than empties it. Joshua is told to meditate in the law day and night. These verses cover what to think about, what Psalm 1 says it grows, and Paul's list in Philippians 4.",
  "short-bible-verses": "Short verses are the ones that stay with you. These are the briefest in the King James Version worth memorizing — a few words each, and most of them carry a whole doctrine.",
  "verses-to-memorize": "These are the passages worth getting into memory first: the ones that answer fear, temptation, guilt and doubt on the spot, when there's no time to go looking. Psalm 119 says the point of hiding the word is having it when you need it.",
  "most-popular-bible-verses": "These are the verses searched, quoted and printed more than any others in the King James Version. Each is quoted in full here with a link to read it in context, which is where most of them get misunderstood.",
  "abraham": "Abraham is called the father of the faithful, and Genesis spends thirteen chapters on him. He left a settled city for a land he had never seen, waited twenty-five years for a promised son, and was counted righteous for believing God long before there was a law to keep. These verses cover his story and what Paul made of it.",
  "adam-and-eve": "The first two chapters of Genesis put Adam and Eve in a garden with one restriction, and the third covers what happened when it was broken. These verses trace the account itself and the New Testament passages that read Christ as the second Adam.",
  "aaron": "Aaron was Moses' older brother, his spokesman before Pharaoh, and Israel's first high priest. He also made the golden calf. Scripture records both without smoothing either one. These verses cover his calling, his failure, and the blessing that still carries his name.",
  "barnabas": "Barnabas means son of consolation, and the record fits the name. He vouched for Paul while the church in Jerusalem was still afraid of him, and later parted ways with Paul over giving John Mark a second chance. These verses cover a man known mostly for believing in people.",
  "boaz": "Boaz appears in the book of Ruth as the kinsman redeemer, the relative with both the right and the willingness to buy back what a family had lost. These verses cover his part in the story and why the New Testament puts him in Christ's genealogy.",
  "caleb": "Caleb was one of two spies out of twelve who came back saying Israel could take the land. Forty-five years later he asked for the hill country, at eighty-five, saying he was as strong as the day Moses sent him out. These verses cover both moments.",
  "cornelius": "Cornelius was a Roman centurion and a Gentile, and the first of them recorded as receiving the gospel. Acts gives a long chapter to it because of how much it changed. These verses cover the vision, Peter's arrival, and what the church concluded afterward.",
  "daniel": "Daniel was carried to Babylon as a young captive and served four kings without bending on the things that mattered. These verses cover his resolve about the king's food, the prayer he kept praying with the window open, and the visions filling the second half of his book.",
  "david": "David is the shepherd who killed Goliath, the king who wrote much of the Psalms, and the man God called after his own heart — who also committed adultery and arranged a murder. Scripture records all of it. These verses cover his life and the songs that came out of it.",
  "david-and-goliath": "A giant held two armies at a standstill for forty days, and a boy delivering bread to his brothers asked why nobody had answered him. These verses cover the whole account in 1 Samuel 17, including the line about the battle being the LORD's.",
  "deborah": "Deborah was a prophetess and the only woman among Israel's judges, holding court under a palm tree and sending Barak into a battle he refused to enter without her. These verses cover the account in Judges 4 and the song in Judges 5.",
  "elijah": "Elijah called down fire on Mount Carmel and then asked God to let him die, inside the space of two chapters. These verses cover the confrontation with Baal's prophets, the still small voice afterward, and the chariot that took him.",
  "elisha": "Elisha asked for a double portion of Elijah's spirit and got it, and the record that follows is full of ordinary need met — a widow's oil, a poisoned pot, a borrowed axe head. These verses cover his calling and his ministry.",
  "enoch": "Enoch gets four verses in Genesis and one of the strangest lines in scripture: he walked with God, and he was not, for God took him. These verses cover what is said about him there and what Hebrews adds later.",
  "esther": "Esther became queen of Persia without anyone knowing she was a Jew, and used the position to stop a planned massacre. God is never named in the book. These verses cover her story and the line about coming to the kingdom for such a time as this.",
  "ezekiel": "Ezekiel was a priest carried off to Babylon who prophesied to people already in exile, often by acting things out rather than only saying them. These verses cover his visions, including the valley of dry bones and the promise of a new heart.",
  "ezra": "Ezra was a scribe who led a group back from exile and set himself to seek the law, to do it, and to teach it, in that order. These verses cover the return, the reading of the law, and the reforms that followed.",
  "gideon": "Gideon was threshing wheat in a winepress to hide it from raiders when he was greeted as a mighty man of valour. He asked for a sign twice. God then cut his army from thirty-two thousand down to three hundred. These verses cover it.",
  "hannah": "Hannah prayed for a son so intensely the priest assumed she was drunk, and when Samuel was born she handed him back. These verses cover her prayer, her promise, and the song she sang that Mary's own song later echoes.",
  "hezekiah": "Hezekiah tore down the high places, trusted God against an Assyrian army already at the gates, and spread a threatening letter out before the LORD. These verses cover his reforms, that siege, and the fifteen years added to his life.",
  "isaac": "Isaac is the son of promise, born to parents far past the age for it, and the boy carrying the wood up the mountain in Genesis 22. These verses cover his birth, that day, and his part in the covenant passed on to Jacob.",
  "isaiah": "Isaiah prophesied for decades and wrote the passages the New Testament quotes most: the child born, the suffering servant, the wings as eagles. These verses cover his call in the temple and the promises that made him the prophet of the gospel.",
  "jacob": "Jacob bought a birthright, stole a blessing, worked fourteen years for a wife, and wrestled with God until his hip gave out and his name changed. These verses cover a man scripture never cleans up and still calls Israel.",
  "jeremiah": "Jeremiah preached for forty years to people who would not listen, and wept about it openly enough to be called the weeping prophet. These verses cover his call, his complaints, and the promises he wrote to exiles who were not going home.",
  "job": "Job lost his children, his property and his health inside two chapters, and spent thirty-five more arguing with friends who were sure he had earned it. God's answer never explains it. These verses cover the book's hardest lines and its best-known ones.",
  "john-the-baptist": "John lived in the wilderness, preached repentance, baptized Jesus, and said he must increase and I must decrease. Jesus called him the greatest born of women. These verses cover his ministry and his end.",
  "jonah": "Jonah ran from a job he did not want, was swallowed and put back on dry land, preached to Nineveh, and then sat down angry when the city repented. The book ends with a question God asks him. These verses cover all four chapters.",
  "jonathan": "Jonathan was Saul's son and heir, and he handed David his robe and his sword knowing exactly what it meant. These verses cover a friendship that cost him a throne and the lament David wrote when he died.",
  "joseph": "Joseph was sold by his brothers, lied about by an employer's wife, forgotten in a prison, and ended up second in Egypt. His line to his brothers — ye thought evil against me, but God meant it unto good — holds the whole story together.",
  "joshua": "Joshua took over from Moses, led Israel across the Jordan and into the land, and made the nation choose at the end of his life. These verses cover his commission, the campaigns, and the line about me and my house.",
  "josiah": "Josiah became king at eight, and at twenty-six had the lost book of the law read to him and tore his clothes at what it said. These verses cover the reforms that followed and the Passover scripture says had no equal since Samuel.",
  "judas-iscariot": "Judas kept the money bag, sold Jesus for thirty pieces of silver, and brought them back before he died. Scripture neither excuses him nor treats him as a puppet. These verses cover what the Gospels actually record.",
  "king-saul": "Saul was Israel's first king, head and shoulders above everybody else, and he lost the throne over partial obedience and a lie about it. These verses cover his anointing, the day Samuel told him obedience is better than sacrifice, and his long decline.",
  "martha-and-mary": "Two sisters, one cooking and one sitting, and Jesus' answer to the complaint has been argued over ever since. These verses cover that afternoon, and the day at their brother's grave when Martha gave one of the clearest confessions in the Gospels.",
  "mary-magdalene": "Mary Magdalene had seven devils cast out of her, followed Jesus to the cross when most had scattered, and was the first person to see him alive. These verses cover what the Gospels actually say about her.",
  "mary-the-mother-of-jesus": "Mary was a young woman in Nazareth told she would bear the Son of God, and her answer was be it unto me according to thy word. These verses cover the annunciation, her song, and the sword Simeon told her about.",
  "melchizedek": "Melchizedek appears in three verses of Genesis as king of Salem and priest of the most high God, with no genealogy given. Hebrews builds a long argument on that silence. These verses cover both passages.",
  "miriam": "Miriam watched her baby brother's basket on the Nile, led Israel's women in song after the sea opened, and was disciplined for speaking against Moses. These verses cover all three.",
  "moses": "Moses was raised in Pharaoh's house, spent forty years herding sheep, and came back at eighty to lead a nation out. He spoke with God face to face and never entered the land himself. These verses cover his call, the law, and his death on the mountain.",
  "naomi": "Naomi went to Moab full and came home empty, asking to be called Mara because of it. The book of Ruth is largely about how that got reversed. These verses cover her grief and the grandson laid in her lap at the end.",
  "nehemiah": "Nehemiah was cupbearer to a Persian king who asked for leave to rebuild a ruined wall and finished it in fifty-two days against constant opposition. These verses cover his prayer, the work, and how he answered the people trying to stop it.",
  "the-prodigal-son": "A son asks for his inheritance early, spends it, and comes home rehearsing a speech he never gets to finish. The father is running before the son reaches the gate. These verses cover Luke 15, including the older brother standing outside, which is the part of the story usually left out.",
  "the-good-samaritan": "A lawyer asked who counted as his neighbor, and Jesus answered with a story where the two religious men walk past and the foreigner stops. These verses cover the parable and the question Jesus turned back on him at the end.",
  "the-good-shepherd": "Jesus called himself the good shepherd and defined it immediately: the one who lays down his life for the sheep, unlike the hireling who runs. These verses cover John 10, Psalm 23, and the shepherd who goes after the one that wandered.",
  "the-lords-prayer": "The disciples asked to be taught to pray and got about sixty words. These verses cover the prayer itself in both Gospel accounts, the line about forgiving that Jesus went back and underlined, and the warning about vain repetition just before it.",
  "the-beatitudes": "Jesus opened the Sermon on the Mount by calling blessed the people nobody would have listed — the poor in spirit, those that mourn, the meek, the persecuted. These verses cover all of them and the reason attached to each.",
  "the-sermon-on-the-mount": "Three chapters of Matthew hold the longest stretch of Jesus' teaching in one place: the beatitudes, salt and light, anger and lust, giving and praying and fasting, worry, judging, and the two houses at the end. These verses cover it.",
  "the-ten-commandments": "Ten commands given at Sinai, four about God and six about people, and Jesus later summed the whole list in two sentences. These verses cover the commandments as Exodus and Deuteronomy give them and what the New Testament does with them.",
  "the-parables-of-jesus": "Jesus taught in stories about soil, seed, coins, sons and servants, and told his disciples plainly that the stories both revealed and concealed. These verses gather the parables and the explanations he gave for a few of them.",
  "the-cross": "These verses cover what happened at the crucifixion and what the New Testament says it accomplished — the price paid, the handwriting blotted out, the veil torn from the top down, and the seven sayings from the cross itself.",
  "the-blood-of-christ": "Scripture treats blood as the price of life, and the whole sacrificial system points forward to one. These verses cover redemption, propitiation, and the passages in Hebrews that explain why the old sacrifices had to keep repeating and this one did not.",
  "the-resurrection": "Paul says that if Christ is not risen the faith is worthless, which is a strange thing to write unless you mean it. These verses cover the empty tomb in all four Gospels, the appearances, and what 1 Corinthians 15 builds on it.",
  "the-ascension": "Forty days after the resurrection, Jesus was taken up while the disciples watched, and two men in white asked why they were still standing there. These verses cover the account and what the New Testament says he is doing now.",
  "the-return-of-christ": "Scripture promises he comes back and refuses to give a date, which is not an oversight. These verses cover the signs, the suddenness, the dead in Christ rising first, and the instruction to watch rather than to calculate.",
  "the-road-to-emmaus": "Two men walked seven miles with a stranger explaining the scriptures about himself, and recognized him only when he broke the bread. These verses cover Luke 24 and the line about their hearts burning while he talked.",
  "the-transfiguration": "Jesus took three disciples up a mountain and changed in front of them, with Moses and Elijah beside him and a voice from a cloud. These verses cover the account in all three Gospels and what Peter wrote about it decades later.",
  "the-twelve-disciples": "Twelve ordinary men — fishermen, a tax collector, a zealot, a traitor — called by name and sent out with authority. These verses cover the calling, the list, the sending, and what became of them.",
  "the-woman-at-the-well": "Jesus went out of his way through Samaria, asked a woman for a drink, and had the longest recorded conversation he had with anyone. These verses cover John 4, the living water, and the town that came out because of her.",
  "the-widows-mite": "A widow put in two mites while the rich were putting in much, and Jesus said she had given more than all of them. These verses cover the account and the standard it sets, which is proportion rather than amount.",
  "walking-on-water": "Jesus came to the disciples on the sea in the fourth watch, and Peter asked to come out. These verses cover the account, the moment Peter saw the wind and began to sink, and the hand that caught him immediately.",
  "jesus-calms-the-storm": "The disciples were bailing water while Jesus slept in the stern, and woke him asking whether he cared. These verses cover the rebuke to the wind, the question he asked them afterward, and what they said about who he must be.",
  "feeding-the-five-thousand": "Five loaves, two fish, and the only miracle recorded in all four Gospels. These verses cover the account, the twelve baskets left over, and what Jesus taught about bread the following day.",
  "daniel-in-the-lions-den": "A law was written specifically to trap Daniel for praying, and he went home and opened his window as usual. These verses cover the den, the king who could not sleep, and the decree that followed in the morning.",
  "the-fiery-furnace": "Three men told a king that their God was able to deliver them, but that they would not bow even if he did not. These verses cover Daniel 3, the furnace heated seven times, and the fourth figure walking in it.",
  "noah": "Noah is called a just man and perfect in his generations, and he built a boat for years while the ground stayed dry. These verses cover the warning, the flood, and the covenant God made afterward with a bow in the cloud.",
  "noahs-ark": "The measurements are given in cubits, the cargo is listed, and the door is shut by God rather than by Noah. These verses cover the building of the ark, the year inside it, and what Peter and Hebrews later made of it.",
  "the-tower-of-babel": "People set out to build a tower to reach heaven and make themselves a name, and left off building when they could no longer understand each other. These verses cover Genesis 11 and the scattering that followed.",
  "the-garden-of-eden": "Two chapters of Genesis describe a garden, a job, a marriage and one restriction, and the third describes what was lost. These verses cover the account, the curse, and the first promise of someone who would bruise the serpent's head.",
  "the-exodus": "A nation walked out of four hundred years of slavery in a single night, through a sea that opened and closed. These verses cover the plagues, the Passover, the crossing, and what the rest of scripture keeps pointing back to it for.",
  "creation": "Genesis opens by assuming God rather than arguing for him, and everything after rests on that first sentence. These verses cover the six days, the Sabbath, and the passages in Job, Psalms and John that look back at the same work.",
  "pentecost": "Fifty days after the resurrection, a sound like wind filled a house, and men who had been hiding preached to a crowd in languages they had not learned. These verses cover Acts 2, Peter's sermon, and the three thousand added that day.",
  "the-church": "The New Testament calls the church a body, a building, a bride and a household, and never a place you go. These verses cover what it is, what it's for, and the instructions given to people trying to be one.",
  "the-bread-of-life": "The day after feeding five thousand, Jesus told the crowd that came looking for more food that he was the bread, and most of them left. These verses cover John 6 and the claim that emptied the room.",
  "the-lamb-of-god": "John the Baptist pointed at Jesus and called him the Lamb of God which taketh away the sin of the world, which would have meant something specific to everyone listening. These verses cover the title from Passover through Revelation.",
  "the-rock": "Scripture calls God a rock more often than almost any other picture — a rock higher than I, a rock that followed them, the rock a wise man built on. These verses gather them, including the stone the builders rejected.",
  "the-word": "John opens his Gospel by calling Jesus the Word, which was with God and was God, and then says the Word was made flesh. These verses cover that title along with what scripture says about the word of God itself.",
  "the-fruit-of-the-spirit": "Paul lists nine things and calls them one fruit, which is the argument in miniature: this grows rather than gets manufactured. These verses cover the list in Galatians 5 and what he sets it against.",
  "the-fear-of-the-lord": "The fear of the LORD is called the beginning of wisdom and the beginning of knowledge, which makes it a starting point rather than a mood. These verses cover what scripture means by it and what it promises to people who have it.",
  "paul": "Paul held the coats at Stephen's stoning, met Christ on the Damascus road, and wrote most of the New Testament from the road and from prison. These verses cover his conversion, his letters, and what he said about the thorn he was never rid of.",
  "peter": "Peter walked on water and sank, swore he'd never deny Jesus and did it three times, and preached the sermon that started the church. These verses cover the whole arc, including the breakfast on the beach where he was restored.",
  "thomas": "Thomas said he would not believe without touching the wounds, and eight days later Jesus showed up and offered them. These verses cover that scene and the confession Thomas made, which is the highest in the Gospel.",
  "nicodemus": "A ruler of the Jews came to Jesus at night and was told he had to be born again. These verses cover that conversation, including John 3:16, and the two later moments where Nicodemus shows up again.",
  "zacchaeus": "A short, wealthy tax collector climbed a tree to see over a crowd, and Jesus stopped underneath it and invited himself to dinner. These verses cover the account and what Zacchaeus did with his money before anybody asked him to.",
  "stephen": "Stephen was chosen to wait tables, preached the longest sermon in Acts, and was stoned praying for the men doing it. These verses cover his defense, his death, and the young man watching who would later be called Paul.",
  "timothy": "Timothy was young, often unwell, and pastoring a difficult church, and Paul wrote him two letters that read like a father's. These verses cover the charge to him and the faith Paul traced back through his mother and grandmother.",
  "ruth": "Ruth was a Moabite widow who refused to leave her mother-in-law, gleaned in a stranger's field, and ended up in the line of David and of Christ. These verses cover her loyalty, the redemption, and the whithersoever thou goest.",
  "abba-father": "Abba is the Aramaic word a child used for his father, and Paul keeps it untranslated twice because no English word carries it. These verses cover the name, the Spirit that prompts it, and what it says about how believers are meant to come to God.",
  "addiction": "Scripture doesn't use the word, but it says a great deal about being mastered by something and about being set free. These verses cover bondage and liberty, the way out Paul promised with every temptation, and God's help for people who cannot stop on their own.",
  "alpha-and-omega": "Alpha and Omega are the first and last letters of the Greek alphabet, and the title appears only in Revelation, spoken by God and by Christ. These verses cover the claim and the ones beside it: the beginning and the ending, which is and which was and which is to come.",
  "angels": "Angels in scripture are messengers and servants, not the sentimental figures of greeting cards, and people who met them tended to fall down. These verses cover what they do, what they are not, and the warning against worshipping them.",
  "anger-management": "The Bible puts limits around anger rather than banning it outright: be ye angry, and sin not, and don't let the sun go down on it. These verses cover slowness to wrath, the soft answer, and what James says about being swift to hear.",
  "beauty": "Scripture's line on beauty is short and steady — favour is deceitful and beauty is vain, and man looks on the outward appearance while God looks on the heart. These verses cover that, along with the beauty the Bible does praise.",
  "betrayal": "The Bible knows betrayal from the inside. David wrote about a familiar friend who lifted up his heel against him, and Jesus quoted that line about Judas. These verses cover being wronged by somebody close and what scripture says to do next.",
  "blessings": "Blessing in scripture is usually spoken over somebody rather than acquired, and it often looks nothing like comfort. These verses cover the blessings promised, the Aaronic blessing pronounced over Israel, and the ones Jesus attached to unlikely people.",
  "brothers-and-sisters": "The Bible's siblings include Cain and Abel, Jacob and Esau, Joseph and his brothers, and Martha and Mary — which is to say scripture is honest about how hard this is. These verses cover those accounts and what is said about family loyalty.",
  "burnout": "Elijah was exhausted and suicidal after his greatest victory, and God fed him and let him sleep before saying anything else. These verses cover rest, limits, and the load Moses was told to stop carrying alone.",
  "business": "Scripture speaks plainly about commerce: a just weight, honest gain, paying a worker the same day, and the trouble with making plans without God in them. These verses cover doing business the way the Bible describes it.",
  "cain-and-abel": "Two brothers brought offerings, one was accepted, and the other killed him in a field. God's question afterward — where is Abel thy brother — is one scripture never lets anyone answer with am I my brother's keeper. These verses cover Genesis 4.",
  "community-and-fellowship": "The New Testament assumes Christians are together, and about fifty of its commands are addressed to one another. These verses cover meeting, bearing, forgiving, and the church in Acts that had all things common.",
  "confession": "To confess is to say the same thing about it that God says, without softening it. These verses cover 1 John's promise, David's psalm after Bathsheba, and James on confessing to one another rather than only privately.",
  "confusion": "Scripture says God is not the author of confusion, which is worth holding when nothing makes sense. These verses cover asking for wisdom, being led in a way you can't see, and the peace that guards a mind while the answer is still missing.",
  "contentment": "Paul says he learned contentment, which means he didn't start with it. He had been full and he had been hungry. These verses cover godliness with contentment as great gain, and the promise that God will never leave, which is the reason attached.",
  "decision-making": "The Bible's guidance passages are less about finding a hidden answer than about who you trust while you choose. These verses cover committing works to the Lord, asking for wisdom, counsel in a multitude, and a lamp that lights one step.",
  "discernment": "Scripture asks Christians to test things rather than accept them — try the spirits, prove all things, hold fast what is good. These verses cover the training of senses Hebrews describes and the Bereans who checked what Paul said against the scriptures.",
  "education-and-learning": "The Bible treats knowledge as good and insufficient on its own: knowledge puffeth up, and the fear of the LORD is where it starts. These verses cover study, teaching, and the difference between knowing things and being wise.",
  "el-shaddai": "El Shaddai is usually rendered God Almighty, and it first appears when God tells a ninety-nine-year-old Abram to walk before him and be perfect. These verses cover the name and the promises attached to it.",
  "emmanuel": "Emmanuel means God with us, given by Isaiah seven centuries before Matthew quoted it at the birth. These verses cover the prophecy, its fulfillment, and the fact that the name is a sentence rather than a title.",
  "exams-and-tests": "Scripture doesn't mention exams and says plenty about work, memory, anxiety and doing your best before God. These verses cover studying as unto the Lord, peace instead of worry, and asking for wisdom without being upbraided for it.",
  "fear-of-death": "Hebrews says Christ took on flesh to free people held all their lifetime in bondage through fear of death, which is a direct statement about this. These verses cover that, the valley with somebody walking through it, and where Paul says the sting went.",
  "freedom": "Freedom in scripture is never freedom from all constraint; it's being released from what had you. These verses cover the truth that makes free, the liberty Christ bought, and Paul's warning not to use it as an occasion for the flesh.",
  "get-well": "These are the verses people write in a card for somebody sick — God near the brokenhearted, the prayer of faith in James, and the psalms that ask for healing plainly without pretending to be cheerful about it.",
  "god-most-high": "El Elyon, God Most High, first appears when Melchizedek blesses Abram, and it shows up again in Daniel and in the psalms. These verses cover a name that says God is over everything else that claims to be in charge.",
  "gods-holiness": "Holy is the only attribute scripture repeats three times in a row, and both Isaiah and John record it that way. These verses cover what God's holiness is, what happened to people who got near it, and what he asks of people called by his name.",
  "good-works": "Ephesians settles the order in two verses: not of works, and then created unto good works. These verses cover that, what James says about faith without them, and Jesus' instruction to let people see them and glorify God rather than you.",
  "government-and-authority": "Scripture tells Christians to submit to governing authorities and also records people who refused an order and were commended. These verses cover both, including Romans 13, praying for kings, and the apostles saying they must obey God rather than men.",
  "health-and-the-body": "The New Testament calls the body a temple and treats it as something on loan rather than owned. These verses cover that, bodily exercise profiting a little, and the care scripture assumes people will take with what they've been given.",
  "hell": "Jesus spoke about hell more than anyone else in the Bible, which is worth noticing. These verses cover what scripture says about judgment and separation from God, stated the way the text states it rather than softened.",
  "holiness": "Be ye holy, for I am holy, is quoted in the New Testament from the Old, which makes it an ongoing command rather than an era. These verses cover being set apart, cleansing, and the holiness Hebrews says is required to see the Lord.",
  "hopelessness": "Scripture is not squeamish about despair. The psalms say things out loud that people are usually afraid to think. These verses cover the honest complaints and the answers that come back, including hope thou in God, said by a man to his own soul.",
  "hospitality": "The New Testament commands hospitality outright, and Hebrews adds that some have entertained angels unawares. These verses cover opening a home, feeding people, and Jesus' instruction to invite the ones who cannot return the favor.",
  "i-am": "God gave Moses the name I AM THAT I AM at the bush, and Jesus used the same words about himself in ways that got him picked up stones. These verses cover both, along with the seven I am sayings in John.",
  "insecurity": "Scripture answers insecurity with facts about who God is rather than pep talk about who you are. These verses cover being made on purpose, chosen, accepted, and the confidence Hebrews says not to cast away.",
  "inspirational": "These are the verses people reach for when they need something to hold on to — the promises about strength, presence, purpose and starting again. Each is quoted in full with a link to read it in context.",
  "jehovah-jireh": "Jehovah Jireh means the LORD will provide, and Abraham named a mountain that after a ram showed up in a thicket at the last possible moment. These verses cover that day and what scripture says elsewhere about God providing.",
  "jehovah-nissi": "Jehovah Nissi means the LORD my banner, and Moses built an altar by that name after Israel's first battle, fought while his hands were held up. These verses cover the account in Exodus 17 and the picture of God as the standard people rally to.",
  "jehovah-rapha": "Jehovah Rapha means the LORD that healeth thee, spoken at bitter water that God made sweet just after the sea opened. These verses cover the name and what scripture says about God healing.",
  "jehovah-shalom": "Jehovah Shalom means the LORD send peace, and Gideon built an altar by that name after being told peace be unto thee while he was afraid for his life. These verses cover the account and the peace God gives.",
  "judgment": "Scripture says everyone gives an account, and it says so to believers and unbelievers in different terms. These verses cover the judgment seat, the books opened, and the sobering line about every idle word.",
  "justice": "The Bible's justice is concrete: honest courts, fair wages, care for the widow and the fatherless, and no partiality for rich or poor. These verses cover what God requires and what he says about those who bend it.",
  "king-of-kings": "King of kings and Lord of lords is used of God in Paul's letter to Timothy and written on Christ's thigh in Revelation. These verses cover the title and the reign it describes.",
  "laziness": "Proverbs is unsparing about the sluggard — the door turning on its hinges, the field grown over, the hand too tired to reach the mouth. These verses cover that and Paul's plain rule about working and eating.",
  "light": "Light runs through scripture from the first day to the last city, and Jesus claims it for himself and then hands it to his followers. These verses cover God as light, Christ as the light of the world, and walking in it.",
  "losing-a-job": "Losing work shakes provision and identity at the same time, and scripture speaks to both. These verses cover God providing, work having worth, and the passages about being sustained in a stretch where the next thing hasn't come.",
  "love": "Love in the Bible is defined by what it does, and the definition comes with God's own action attached — herein is love, not that we loved him. These verses cover love for God, for neighbors, for enemies, and 1 Corinthians 13 in full.",
  "lust-and-purity": "Jesus moved the line from the act to the look, which made purity a matter of the heart rather than the record. These verses cover fleeing, guarding the eyes, and the promise to the pure in heart.",
  "mentoring-and-discipleship": "The New Testament's pattern is one person teaching another who will teach somebody else — Paul to Timothy to faithful men. These verses cover following, teaching, and the commission to make disciples rather than converts.",
  "miracles": "The Bible's miracles cluster around a few moments rather than running evenly through it, and they always point somewhere. These verses cover what God did, what Jesus did, and what John said the signs were written down for.",
  "morning": "Scripture puts a lot in the morning: mercies new, David's voice heard early, Jesus up before daylight to pray. These verses cover starting a day with God and the psalms written to be prayed then.",
  "neighbors": "Love thy neighbour as thyself is the second of the two commands Jesus said the law hangs on, and when he was asked who qualified he told a story about a Samaritan. These verses cover both.",
  "overcoming": "Overcoming in scripture is never done unaided — they overcame by the blood of the Lamb, and this is the victory, even our faith. These verses cover the promises to those who overcome and the source the Bible keeps crediting.",
  "persecution": "Jesus told his followers to expect it and called them blessed in it, which is not the same as telling them to enjoy it. These verses cover being hated for his name, rejoicing in it, and praying for those who do it.",
  "positivity": "Scripture's version of this is not optimism; it's fixing the mind on what's true. Paul lists what to think on and tells people to set their affection on things above. These verses cover that, along with the joy and hope the Bible actually promises.",
  "prince-of-peace": "Prince of Peace is the last of the four names Isaiah gives the child, and the peace in view is between God and people before it's anything else. These verses cover the title and the peace Jesus said he was leaving behind.",
  "priscilla-and-aquila": "Priscilla and Aquila were tentmakers, Paul's coworkers, and a married couple who took Apollos aside and explained the way of God more perfectly to him. These verses cover their work and the church that met in their house.",
  "prophecy": "Scripture treats fulfilled prophecy as evidence and unfulfilled prophecy as a reason to be ready. These verses cover the prophecies about Christ, the test of a true prophet, and Peter's note that prophecy never came by the will of man.",
  "rahab": "Rahab hid two spies in Jericho, hung a scarlet cord from her window, and ends up in Hebrews 11 and in Christ's genealogy. These verses cover the account and what James and Hebrews both made of her.",
  "reconciliation": "The New Testament uses the word first of God and people and then of people with each other, in that order and on purpose. These verses cover being reconciled to God, the ministry of reconciliation, and Jesus' instruction to leave the gift at the altar and go make it right.",
  "rejection": "Isaiah said the Messiah would be despised and rejected, and the Gospels record his own town running him out. These verses cover being turned away, the stone the builders refused, and the acceptance scripture says doesn't depend on people.",
  "renewing-your-mind": "Paul's word is transformed, and he says it happens by the renewing of the mind rather than by trying harder. These verses cover what to put in, what to put off, and the thinking Philippians 4 prescribes.",
  "respect": "Scripture asks for honor in specific directions — parents, elders, leaders, employers, spouses, and everybody as made in God's image. These verses cover honour all men and the commandment with promise attached.",
  "revenge": "The Bible takes vengeance away from people and puts it somewhere else: vengeance is mine, I will repay, saith the Lord. These verses cover that, not rendering evil for evil, and overcoming evil with good instead.",
  "samson": "Samson had strength nobody could match and almost no control over himself, and the last thing he did was the greatest. These verses cover his birth, his failures, and the prayer he prayed between two pillars.",
  "samuel": "Samuel was given back to God as a boy, heard his name called in the night, anointed Israel's first two kings, and told Saul that obedience is better than sacrifice. These verses cover his life and his warnings.",
  "sarah": "Sarah laughed when she overheard that she would have a son at ninety, and then had one and named him laughter. These verses cover the promise, the waiting, and the faith Hebrews credits her with.",
  "sleep": "Scripture connects sleep to trust: I will both lay me down in peace and sleep, for thou only makest me dwell in safety. These verses cover rest at night, God giving his beloved sleep, and the anxiety that keeps people awake.",
  "solomon": "Solomon asked for an understanding heart instead of riches and got both, built the temple, wrote much of Proverbs, and then let his own wisdom slip. These verses cover the request, the reign, and the conclusion of Ecclesiastes.",
  "speaking-life": "Proverbs says death and life are in the power of the tongue, which is a heavier claim than people usually mean by it. These verses cover words that build, words that destroy, and the fact that James spends a whole chapter on it.",
  "spiritual-dryness": "The psalms have a language for this that most people never learned — my soul thirsteth, as the hart panteth, how long wilt thou forget me. These verses cover the dry stretches and the God who is still there in them.",
  "success": "Scripture's success is measured differently and tied to a specific habit: Joshua was told to meditate in the law day and night and then he would prosper. These verses cover that, committing works to God, and the warning about gaining the world.",
  "surgery": "These are the verses people pray before an operation, for themselves or somebody they love — fear not, for I am with thee, the God who made the body, and the peace that keeps a mind while the waiting room clock moves slowly.",
  "sympathy": "These are the verses to send when somebody has lost a person — the shepherd psalm, the God of all comfort, sorrowing but not without hope, and the promise that he wipes away the tears himself.",
  "talents-and-gifts": "The New Testament says every believer got something and none of it was for themselves. These verses cover the gift lists, the parable of the talents, and Peter's line about ministering it one to another as good stewards.",
  "the-names-of-god": "God's names in scripture are descriptions rather than labels — provider, healer, banner, peace, shepherd, almighty, I AM. These verses gather them with the passages where each one was given.",
  "the-names-of-jesus": "Scripture gives Jesus dozens of names and titles, and each one is a claim: Emmanuel, the Word, the Lamb of God, the good shepherd, the bright and morning star, King of kings. These verses cover them in the places they appear.",
  "the-poor": "The Bible says more about the poor than most readers expect, and it is rarely advice. These verses cover lending to the LORD by giving to them, the judgment in Matthew 25, and the instruction not to show partiality to the rich.",
  "the-trinity": "Scripture never uses the word and states the pieces plainly: one God, and the Father, the Son and the Holy Ghost each spoken of as God. These verses gather the passages the doctrine is drawn from, including the baptism and the great commission.",
  "time": "The Bible treats time as short and given — numbering days, redeeming the time, a season for everything. These verses cover what scripture says about how long a life is and what to do with it.",
  "travel": "These are the verses people pray before a trip: the God who keeps your going out and your coming in, the angels charged to keep you in all thy ways, and the promise of a presence that goes whithersoever thou goest.",
  "verses-for-cards": "Short, complete verses that fit on a card and say something worth saying — for a birth, a wedding, a loss, a hard stretch, or no occasion at all. Each is quoted in full with a link to read it in context.",
  "verses-for-new-believers": "These are the passages worth reading first: what happened, what is true now, and what to do next. They cover assurance, forgiveness, the new birth, and the plain instructions the New Testament gives somebody at the start.",
};
